🧾 [1] User sends /signin request
🔐 [2] Calls this line: authenticationManager.authenticate( new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(username, password));
⚙️ [4] Spring Internally → calls:   → DaoAuthenticationProvider.authenticate()
                                    → calls userDetailsService.loadUserByUsername()
                                    → returns UserDetailsImpl (with username, password, authorities)
                                    → Password is verified using PasswordEncoder.matches()
//This filter{ http.addFilterBefore(authenticationJwtTokenFilter(), UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class);}
    :Extracts JWT from header | Verifies it using your JwtUtils.validateJwtToken(...)
If valid: -> Loads user using UserDetailsService.loadUserByUsername(...) | Creates auth object and sets it into SecurityContextHolder

🧠 [5] If correct: Authentication object is returned : Authentication auth = UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(userDetails, null, authorities);
💾 [6] Set it in SecurityContextHolder : SecurityContextHolder.getContext().setAuthentication(auth);
    SecurityContextHolder.setAuthentication() is done temporarily just for this request.Once the /signin response is returned, that SecurityContext is gone.
🔑 [7] JWT generated and 📦 [8] JWT returned to client

🔐 PROTECTED REQUEST FLOW (e.g., /admin)

🚀 [9] Client sends request with token
    So internally Spring Security configures this at application startup, then applies it to every request.
🔎 [10] Request hits your AuthTokenFilter (from addFilterBefore(...)) directly as spring security do firewall check internally
            doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res, FilterChain chain)
    Inside this :      → parseJwt(req)               // extract token from header
                        → jwtUtils.validateJwtToken(jwt)
                        → jwtUtils.getUserNameFromJwtToken(jwt)
                        → userDetailsService.loadUserByUsername(username)
                        → new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(userDetails, null, authorities)
                        → SecurityContextHolder.getContext().setAuthentication(auth)
                        → chain.doFilter()
✅ [11] Now request goes to Controller : Spring checks user’s roles via SecurityContextHolder.getContext().getAuthentication() and grants or denies access.
SecurityContextHolder.setAuthentication() is called again.
